      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rainsburg derives its name from pioneer hunter John Rains, who settled there in the early 1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bedford250.com/History250TownshipsVIII.htm",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1800, James Donahoe opened a store, hotel, and tannery that became the town’s first businesses.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bedford250.com/History250TownshipsVIII.htm",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Rainsburg once housed a tannery and a creamery, but today it is almost entirely residential.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bedford250.com/History250TownshipsVIII.htm",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Samuel Williams founded the Allegheny Male and Female Seminary in 1853, raising funds by selling 138 shares of stock.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainsburg%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Rainsburg uniquely hosts churches for three Methodist denominations—Episcopal, South, and Protestant—each with its own historic building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainsburg%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town’s roots trace back to Pennsylvania Germans, including Lutheran, German Reformed, and Mennonite communities that still thrive nearby.",
